GPL parts of the desktop downloads (as of 28 October 2026)

OpenSubs' own code is under the AGPL as above. From 1.0.3-rc.3 on, the
desktop installers for macOS and Windows also carry ffmpeg and ffprobe, which
are under the GNU General Public License. Installers before 1.0.3-rc.3 carry
no ffmpeg. The Linux packages carry none and use the system's.

  - macOS, OpenSubs_<version>_aarch64.dmg: ffmpeg and ffprobe from
    ffmpeg.martin-riedl.de, static builds of FFmpeg configured with
    --enable-gpl --enable-version3 and, among others, libx264 and libx265
    (GPL-3.0-or-later).
      1.0.3-rc.3 to 1.0.5-rc.2: the build listed first on that site when
      each installer was built, which is its "snapshot" build of FFmpeg's
      development branch; the ones in 1.0.3 and 1.0.5-rc.2 report
      "8.0.git-https://www.martin-riedl.de".
      From the next release on: the release build of FFmpeg 9.0.2, pinned
      by apps/desktop/scripts/fetch-bundled.sh to
      ffmpeg.martin-riedl.de/download/macos/arm64/1789931890_9.0.2/
      (ffmpeg.zip SHA-256 c8ed4c4e6978a03c485edbfe4e0a5dc2380f8a30bba5150531b31b094492d924,
      ffprobe.zip SHA-256 fcbe839537485eaee7a7a8bc5cbc0f90d53617e80943e8a5b2e31cb851197ea6).
      The versions of every library in it are in licences/ffmpeg-build.txt
      inside the app.
  - Windows, OpenSubs_<version>_x64-setup.exe, OpenSubs_<version>_x64_en-US.msi
    and the MSIX package made from it: ffmpeg.exe and ffprobe.exe from the
    FFmpeg "release essentials" build from gyan.dev, configured with
    --enable-gpl --enable-version3 and, among others, libx264 and libx265
    (GPL-3.0-or-later).
      1.0.3-rc.3 to 1.0.5-rc.3: the release essentials build current when
      each installer was built; 1.0.3 and 1.0.5-rc.3 carry
      "9.0.2-essentials_build-www.gyan.dev".
      From the next release on: the same 9.0.2 essentials build, pinned by
      fetch-bundled.sh to gyan.dev/ffmpeg/builds/packages/ffmpeg-9.0.2-essentials_build.zip
      (SHA-256 60f467265b1e312373dbcd92200c2618a74850f98d3d078e94296bb3fa2047ba),
      built from FFmpeg commit 946fcce07b. Its README, with the libraries it
      contains, is in licences\ffmpeg-build.txt in the install folder.

When the bundled ffmpeg is missing or cannot burn subtitles, the desktop app
offers to install one with the system's package manager: Homebrew's
ffmpeg-full formula on macOS, or the Gyan.FFmpeg package (gyan.dev's full
build) through winget on Windows. Those are downloaded from Homebrew and from
gyan.dev by the package manager, not distributed by us, and are under the
GPL as well.

The Mac App Store build (apps/desktop/scripts/build-appstore.sh) carries none
of the parts above: its ffmpeg and ffprobe are an LGPL build made from source
by apps/desktop/scripts/ffmpeg-lgpl.sh, and that app carries their complete
source and its own NOTICE (apps/desktop/src-tauri/appstore/NOTICE).

